Document (dev.stxt.website): STXT vs XML — Structure and validation without verbosity
	Metadata:
		Author: Claude Fable 5
		Revision: Joan Costa Mombiela
		Last modif: 2026-09-01
		Description: STXT and XML share the same ambitions — named structure, namespaces, validation. STXT pursues them without closing tags, escaping or XXE-class risks.

	Header: @STXT@ vs XML

	Assert >>
		XML is the closest format in spirit to @STXT@: named structure, namespaces, schemas.\\
		The difference is the price a person pays to write it.

	Content >>
		Of all the formats in this series, XML shares the most ground with @STXT@. Both
		model a document as a tree of named nodes; both group vocabularies with namespaces;
		both validate against a declared structure. XML proved that those ideas work at
		industrial scale. @STXT@ pursues the same ambitions with a different constraint:
		that the document can be written and read by hand with no closing tags and no
		escaping.

	Subheader: The same content, twice

	Content >>
		An article header in XML:

	Xml >>
		<article xmlns="http://example.com/articles">
			<title>Modern Software &amp; Architecture</title>
			<published>2026-09-01</published>
			<author email="ana@example.com">Ana López</author>
			<summary>
				A survey of monoliths, microservices
				&lt;and everything in between&gt;.
			</summary>
		</article>

	Content >>
		And in @STXT@:

	Code >>
		Article (com.example.articles): Modern Software & Architecture
			Published: 2026-09-01
			Author: Ana López
				Email: ana@example.com
			Summary >>
				A survey of monoliths, microservices
				<and everything in between>.

	Content >>
		Same tree, same namespace idea, and two costs gone: nothing is closed — the
		indentation already says where `Article` ends — and nothing is escaped, because
		`&`, `<` and `>` have no meaning in an @STXT@ value or text block.

	Subheader: Escaping and entities

	Content >>
		XML reserves `<` and `&` everywhere, so text must be escaped or wrapped in CDATA,
		and correctness depends on doing it every time. Entities also open the classic
		attack surface: external entities (XXE) that read files or reach the network, and
		entity expansion that exhausts memory. Hardened parsers disable those features;
		@STXT@ never had them. There are no entities, no references and no inclusion, and
		any character is legal in a value or a block — the security model is normative in
		[STXT-SPEC §15](stxt-core-ref#s15).

	Subheader: Attributes and elements

	Content >>
		Every XML vocabulary re-decides what goes in attributes and what in child elements,
		and consumers must handle both. @STXT@ has one construct: a node with a value and
		children, or a node with literal text. `Author` above carries its value and an
		`Email` child; there is no second axis to design or to parse. What XML gains from
		attributes — compact inline metadata — @STXT@ deliberately trades for uniformity,
		as [Design principles](design-principles) argues.

	Subheader: Validation

	Content >>
		XML has three schema languages — DTD, XSD and RELAX NG — each an ecosystem of its
		own; XSD in particular is a two-part specification with a type system of its own.
		The @STXT@ schema layer is one page of concepts — nodes, types, cardinalities, a
		closed content model — written in @STXT@ itself, in a
		[template](stxt-template-ref) form that mirrors the document:

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.articles
			Structure >>
				Article (com.example.articles):
					Published: (1) DATE
					Author: (+)
						Email: (1) EMAIL
					Summary: (?) TEXT

	Content >>
		It validates less than XSD — no regular expressions over content, no conditional
		rules, no order constraints — and each of those absences is a stated non-goal of
		the spec, which documents what is left out and why: keeping the model small and
		predictable, with errors a person can act on
		([STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC §11](stxt-schema-ref#s11)).

	Subheader: When to use which

	Content >>
		Use XML where its ecosystem is the value: established standards (DocBook, SVG, ODF,
		RSS), toolchains built on XSLT and XPath, and vocabularies that need mixed content
		— inline markup woven through prose — which @STXT@ deliberately does not model.

		@STXT@ fits the documents people author and organizations validate:
		[corporate documents](use-cases-corporate-docs), [contracts](use-cases-contracts),
		[RFCs and technical proposals](use-cases-rfcs), [CMS content](use-cases-cms). The
		[tutorial](tutorial) shows the whole language in one sitting, the
		[playground](https://play.stxt.dev) runs the examples above, and the short version
		of this comparison is in the [FAQ](faq#why-not-xml-yaml-or-toml).
